  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: PY_LOOPS
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: PY_LOOPS
  language: 1
  conceptTitle: 'Loops: for and range'
  symbol: for
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - PY_07_LOOPS
  - HZ_PY_04_LOOPS
  - PY_RUN_04_LOOPS
  - PY_RUN_11_LOOPS_SUM
  topics:
  - Loops
  definition: A loop repeats a block of code, so you don't have to write the same
    lines again and again.
  howItWorks: A for loop runs once for every value in a sequence. range(n) makes
    the numbers 0, 1, 2 ... up to n - 1, so it gives n values. The lines to repeat
    are indented under the for line, and the for line ends with a colon.
  exampleCode: "for star in range(2):\n    print(\"Twinkle\")\nprint(\"Done\")"
  exampleExplanation: range(2) gives 0 and 1 - two values - so the indented line
    runs two times and prints Twinkle twice. print("Done") is not indented, so it
    runs once, after the loop has finished.
  tip: range(n) starts at 0 and stops just before n.
